---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Badge from "@/components/ui/Badge.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { REPO, repoDoc } from "@/lib/site";
---

<Docs title="Getting started" description="Run a Worker and its Durable Objects with cellb in Node and in a browser.">
  <Badge variant="brand" class="mb-4">v0.0.1 · not yet published to npm</Badge>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    cellb (<code>@statewalker/cellb</code>) is a Durable Objects runtime written in TypeScript. It
    runs the same Workers code in a browser and in Node. A Worker module and its Durable Object
    classes get synchronous SQLite storage, input and output gates, stubs with
    <code>fetch</code> and RPC, alarms, WebSockets, Worker Loaders and facets, all without
    modification.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    The package lives in the <a href={REPO}>cellb.dev monorepo</a> and has no build step yet. Its
    entry points are TypeScript sources, so import them through a bundler, Vite or vitest. Node
    22.13 or later is needed for <code>node:sqlite</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`git clone ${REPO}\ncd cellb.dev\npnpm install`} />

  <h2>Three entry points</h2>
  <p>Callers differ by platform, so the package is split by platform:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Import</th><th>What it gives</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>@statewalker/cellb</code></td><td>The isomorphic runtime: <code>createCellRuntime</code>, <code>createProjectRuntime</code>, <code>DurableObject</code>, <code>WorkerEntrypoint</code>, <code>celldBucket</code>, the object stores, links over ports, <code>CellRouter</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>@statewalker/cellb/node</code></td><td><code>nodeSqliteOpener</code>, <code>sqliteObjectStore</code>, <code>loadNodeProject</code>, D1 migrations, and celld's peer protocol</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>@statewalker/cellb/browser</code></td><td><code>browserProjectRuntime</code>, <code>sahpoolOpener</code>, <code>createProjectSite</code>, <code>serviceWorkerConnector</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A Durable Object in Node</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    title="counter.ts"
    code={`import { createCellRuntime, DurableObject } from "@statewalker/cellb";
import { nodeSqliteOpener } from "@statewalker/cellb/node";

class Counter extends DurableObject {
  increment(): number {
    const sql = this.ctx.storage.sql;
    sql.exec("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS c (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, n INTEGER)");
    sql.exec("INSERT INTO c (id, n) VALUES (1, 1) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET n = n + 1");
    return sql.exec<{ n: number }>("SELECT n FROM c WHERE id = 1").one().n;
  }
}

const runtime = createCellRuntime({
  worker: {
    Counter,
    default: {
      fetch: async (req: Request, env: any) =>
        Response.json({ n: await env.COUNTER.getByName(new URL(req.url).pathname).increment() }),
    },
  },
  durableObjects: { COUNTER: { className: "Counter" } },
  openSql: nodeSqliteOpener("./data"), // a file per cell; () keeps them in memory
});

const res = await runtime.fetch(new Request("http://local/counter/a")); // {"n":1}
await runtime.close();`}
  />
  <p>
    A runtime is a plain <code>(Request) ⇒ Response</code> handler. Mount
    <code>runtime.fetch</code> on <code>node:http</code>, a ServiceWorker or a test, and call
    <code>runtime.close()</code> when you are done. Closing takes final backups and closes every
    database.
  </p>

  <h2>The same project in a browser</h2>
  <p>
    In a browser, cells live in SQLite WASM on OPFS inside a Dedicated Worker, and requests arrive
    through a ServiceWorker.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    title="worker.ts"
    code={`import sqlite3InitModule from "@sqlite.org/sqlite-wasm";
import { browserProjectRuntime } from "@statewalker/cellb/browser";

const host = await browserProjectRuntime({
  config,            // the parsed wrangler.jsonc
  code: { bundle },  // main, bundled for browsers (or { site } for the in-browser HTTP server)
  sqlite3: await sqlite3InitModule(),
  pool: "my-app",    // the OPFS SQLite pool; one per worker
  filesDir: "my-app-files",
});
const res = await host.runtime.fetch(new Request("http://browser/"));`}
  />

  <h2>Join a celld fleet</h2>
  <p>
    Give the runtime a <code>celldBucket</code> and it becomes a node of a celld fleet. It uses the
    same bucket layout, ownership records and LTX log, so a cell can move between celld and cellb
    with no conversion.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    code={`const { runtime, unsupported } = await loadNodeProject("./counter", {
  openSql: nodeSqliteOpener(),
  files: new MemFilesApi(),
  storage: celldBucket(sqliteObjectStore("./counter/.celld/dev/objects.sqlite3"), { node: "cellb-1" }),
});
console.log(unsupported); // configuration cellb does not support, e.g. []`}
  />

